{"message": "Kinetic sculpture — big, walk-in-scale, built from salvaged industrial steel and thick plate glass. Not the polished gallery kind with a motor and a nice hum. I want pieces driven by thermal expansion, slow hydraulics, and the weight of water, moving so slowly that a visitor standing there for a minute genuinely can't tell whether it's moving. You'd have to come back the next day to be sure.

The theme I'd actually commit to is maintenance. I'd build deliberate wear into the work — a bronze bearing designed to grind itself oval, a cable that's meant to fray on a schedule — and the piece would only stay whole if someone kept tending it. I like that because it makes care part of the artwork rather than something that happens to it. Most sculpture pretends to be permanent and then rots in a crate; I'd rather make the rot the honest subject and put a wrench in the visitor's hand.

The reason this excites me more than anything on a screen is that it can fail. A painting is finished and safe forever. A machine that has to be kept alive is a real thing in the world, with a schedule and a smell and a person who has to show up."}

